  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Prolonged stretch of dry weather across southern New \nEngland, potentially lasting through Halloween. Afternoon \ntemperatures moderate through Monday, followed by a brief cooldown \nTuesday and Wednesday before milder weather returns late in the week.\n\nIf the forecast discussion starts sounding like a broken record over \nthe next several days, you can thank strong high pressure for that! \nAn extended stretch of dry weather is expected, with some day-to-day \nvariability in temperatures, but otherwise not much excitement on \nthe horizon. In fact, global ensembles paint a rather parched \npicture for southern New England, with cumulative probabilities of \nmeasurable precipitation (0.01 inches or greater) generally less \nthan 10 percent through much of the upcoming week. Fingers crossed \nthis dry pattern holds just a little longer and gives us a dry All \nHallows' Eve! The next opportunity for precipitation may arrive \ntoward the end of next weekend, as global guidance hints at a \ntransition toward a more active weather pattern during the first \nweek of November.\n\nAn expansive area of surface high pressure, around 1035 mb, remains \nanchored across the region. This will promote plenty of sunshine, \nlight winds, and generally quiet conditions. Temperatures at 925 mb \nwarm to +10C today and +14C Monday. This should support highs in the \nlower to middle 60s today, with some locations potentially reaching \n70F Monday. A moisture-starved cold front approaches from the north-\nnorthwest Monday night into early Tuesday. However, persistent high \npressure and dry air will likely limit the front to little more than \nsome additional cloud cover and a cooler air mass. Highs return to \nthe 50s Tuesday and Wednesday before potentially rebounding into the \n60s Thursday through Saturday with return flow out of the west-\nsouthwest. DESI probabilities show low to moderate chances of highs \nFriday afternoon reaching 70F or higher, generally between 30 and 50 \npercent. \n\nAs for overnight temperatures, many locations are waking up to a \nfrosty landscape this morning, with temperatures in the 20s and \nlower 30s. Similar conditions are possible Monday morning, although \ntemperatures should be somewhat \"warmer,\" with lows generally in the \nupper 20s to upper 30s. Overnight lows trend closer to seasonable \nlevels in the 40s through much of the upcoming week, potentially \nreaching the middle to upper 40s by week's end. One exception may be \nWednesday morning, when a cooler post-frontal air mass, combined \nwith light winds and mainly clear skies, could allow temperatures to \nfall back into the 20s and lower 30s. Confidence remains somewhat \nlower given uncertainty surrounding cloud cover and winds, both of \nwhich could limit radiational cooling. Be sure to protect those mums \nand Halloween pumpkins!",
